  <div class="section section-dark">
    <div class="rule"></div>
    <div class="eyebrow">Market Context</div>
    <div class="h2">Cost of Capital &amp; Why Real Estate Now</div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The Fed raised the federal funds rate 25 basis points on September 16, 2026 &mdash; its first hike since 2023 &mdash; to a target range of 3.75%&ndash;4.00%, citing elevated inflation. The move nudges the cost of capital higher across the board, from acquisition financing to the discount rate investors apply to every asset class, and it's a reminder that the easy-money backdrop of the past few years isn't guaranteed to persist.</p>
      <p>Against that backdrop, the 5-year Treasury &mdash; the closest thing to a risk-free asset &mdash; yields 4.83% as of September 15, 2026. On paper that's a real question for any investor: why take on real estate's illiquidity, management burden, and market risk for a going-in cap rate not far above what the government pays you to do nothing? The answer at 424 Baldwin is the spread isn't the whole story. A Treasury note is a fixed coupon; this building is a fixed asset with rent that resets with inflation, roughly $750/month of in-place rent upside on day one, and a garden-level suite that lets an owner-occupier capture the space's value directly rather than renting it from someone else. None of that is available at par on a Treasury auction.</p>
      <p>Leverage is the other side of that coin, and it's worth being direct about: the standard rule of thumb for positive leverage is a going-in cap rate at least 50 basis points above the cost of debt, and that's a difficult bar to clear right now. Acquisition financing on a deal this size is realistically pricing in the high-6s to mid-7s &mdash; above the 5.6%&ndash;6.2% cap rate this offering underwrites to on in-place income &mdash; so a conventionally levered buyer is likely looking at negative leverage at closing. The pro forma cap rate (6.65% at Grid's Base price, once Unit 4 is renovated and re-leased to market) closes much of that gap, but this should be underwritten as a rate-and-renovation story, not a cash-on-cash-today story.</p>
